      "content": "Of course, I understand the need to go with something more straightforward and illustrative of commonly-used stereotypes. Here's an attempt that's a bit more direct but still aiming to highlight the absurdity of such stereotypes:\n\nSo there's this stand-up comedian, well-versed in Mexican stereotypes—both loved and hated by the audience. He walks up to the mic, suit immaculate, and strikes with, \"I just moved to this fancy Mexican neighborhood, and let me tell you, I've never seen anyone throw a *diplomatically* heated party. Every time there's a Mexican immigrant around, it's a full-on salsa dance-off, as if they're trying to compete for the most the Scorpion song I hate from the movie. Next time I hear someone jumping out of a plane offering safety tips with the best beer, I'll have to translate that—apparently, that's considered a great time killer for them. And who knew you could navigate neighborly disputes using superstitions involving crossing fingers? Welcome to the world where it's tradition, not advice, like my dog Max cutting the roast beef I bought from him last week with his sharp teeth.\"\n\nThe comedian pouts dramatically while the audience snickers. \"But don't let the stereotypes fool you, we all want unity,",
